  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale\nThis approach combines smooth optimization, Fourier analysis, and multi-resolution scanning to break free from local optima that plagued the previous solution. The key idea is to represent the periodic step function in the frequency domain, exploit cross-scale relationships, and use a continuous relaxation to escape the minima found by step-function approaches. This is motivated by mathematical knowledge that optimal solutions frequently show periodic properties and spectral concentration. The primary limitation is that Fourier transformations may not perfectly capture piecewise constant structures, but this is mitigated with phase adjustment and amplitude inversions.\n\nMathematical Specification\n1. Representation: The candidate solution is parameterized by Fourier coefficients (real and imaginary parts) for frequencies up to k_max. The physical domain signal is reconstructed by inverse Fourier transform and then rounded to [0,1] values, normalizing to meet the integral constraint.\n\n2. Variables:\n   - n_bases: Number of Fourier basis functions (periods)\n   - coeffs: Vector of complex coefficients (magnitude and phase) for each basis function\n   - dx: Step width (2/n_points)\n   - n_points: Resolution (power-of-two for FFT efficiency)\n\n3. Objective: \n   C5 = (2/n_points) * max_k [cross_correlation(h(x), 1-h(x+k))]\n\n4. Constraints:\n   - Physical domain: 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 for all x\n   - Integral normalization: dx * \u2211h(x) = 1\n\n5. Frequency-space Transformation:\n   - Fourier coefficients are projected to |c_k| \u2264 c_max_k (depending on scale)\n   - Boundary forcing at low frequencies (k=0) to maintain smoothness\n\nImplementation Procedure\n1. Initialization:\n   - Default resolution: 64 points\n   - Alternative resolutions: Powers of two (64, 128, 256, 512)\n   - Start with random coefficients (rescaled magnitude)\n\n2. Core Optimization:\n   a. Fourier \u2192 Physical transformation using inverse FFT\n   b. Value clipping and integral normalization (using a reproject function)\n   c. Fourier \u2192 Frequency derivative computation via differentiation in physical space\n   d. Project gradient back to frequency space\n   e. Update with conjugate gradient descent (Polak-Ribi\u00e8re) with adaptive time stepping\n\n3. Multi-Scale Exploration:\n   For each resolution:\n   - Define k_max_limit from sqrt(n_points) to n_points//2\n   - Compute objective via physical domain (pyfunc) and cache\n   - Use frequency space gradient with finite-difference fallback for boundaries\n\n4. Search Choices:\n   - Gradual phase unwrapping (20% chance per iteration)\n   - Coefficient magnitude randomization (10% chance)\n   - Periodic pruning to keep distinct frequency bands\n\n5. Budget Handling:\n   - Set stages:\n     - Initialization: 5%\n     - Main optimization: 80%\n     - Local search: 10%\n     - Validation: 5%\n   - Use jax auto-diff if available for gradient estimation\n\n6. Validation:\n   - Check physical domain [0,1] bounds via histogram\n   - Recompute integral via multiple sample points to verify\n   - Warnings/Fallback: Use greedy rounding if NaN found\n\nSearch choices and useful variations:\n1. Resolution progression: 64 \u2192 128 \u2192 256 \u2192 512 (scaled dx)\n2. Phase unwrapping strategy (floor atan2(\u2111/\u211c)/window_size)\n3. Randomized coefficient projection (0.05 \u03b1*Euclidean norm)\n\nComputebudget and robustness:\n- n_points = 64 (base)\n- FFT computations (O(n log n)\n- Derivatives via central difference (O(n\u00b2))\n- Time estimates: 500 seconds baseline (1300s total for 4 stages)\n- Graceful degradation: Fix with Metropolis Monte Carlo if gradient failures occur\n\nValidation and return contract:\n- Output format: (h_physical_array, objective_value, n_points)\n- Throw if integral doesn\u2019t match (1e-13 tolerance)\n- Triple-check objective via normalized cross-correlation\n- Fallback structure: Use optimal continuous relaxation if not progress after 25% of max_iter",
